for years I've been talking about the  importance of the 2020 census really I  guess since 2010 since the last census  and the census is so important because  of gerrymandering and redistricting the  census determines how significant  billions of dollars in government  funding will be distributed and it also  helps us understand how the country is  changing in terms of family size in  terms of urban versus rural living I  mean there's just so many reasons that  an accurate census is important and  we're potentially heading towards a  major disaster with the 2020 census and  many people have no idea that this is  happening there are a number of  different problems that are leading us  to a potentially destructive 2020 census  first of all in 2010 Congress decided  that the 2020 census shouldn't cost any  more than the 2010 census red flag right  number one in ten years there's been  inflation so even if you want the 2020  census to cost quote the same as the  2010 census in inflation-adjusted  dollars you've got to adjust for  inflation that hasn't been done but also  the population is going to be bigger in  2020 than it was in 2010 if you have  more people to count it's sort of  obvious that it's going to cost more or  you should devote more money in order to  do an equally good job if there are more  people to count that's number one but it  gets even worse earlier this year Donald  Trump made more cuts to the Census  Bureau which is going to make the  problem even more acute now for  comparison the census funding jumped 96  percent from 1997 to 1998 census funding  went up more than sixty percent from  2007 to 2008 and now we are saying we're  gonna cut funding for the Census and the  director of the Census Bureau decided to  resign as a result of this a replacement  has of course not been nominated not  only do you need money to carry out the  census successfully you need a director  of the Census Bureau to carry out a  successful census and some of the  reasons 